# Nothing is ignored here without a reason stated next to it. If you cannot say
# why a path must stay out of the repo, it should be committed — an over-broad
# ignore silently hides files a shared repo is supposed to share, and git never
# descends into an excluded directory, so a later exception cannot undo it.

# Per-machine state this repo writes itself: which profile is bound to which
# Claude home on THIS machine (local/.homes), and the legacy .current-profile.
# The repo defines profiles; each machine declares which homes exist.
local/

# Created by the OS and by editors without being asked, never intentional content.
.DS_Store
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Claude Code's project directory. Only the per-machine parts are ignored — the
# rest of `.claude/` is meant to be shared. Per the Claude Code docs,
# `.claude/settings.json` is "checked into source control and shared with your
# team", while `.claude/settings.local.json` is not; project skills, subagents,
# and slash commands under `.claude/` track with the repo the same way.
# https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings
#
# settings.local.json is where Claude Code records permanent permission grants,
# so it is the one file here that accumulates machine-specific commands. Claude
# Code gitignores it automatically when it creates the file; this covers creating
# it by hand. worktrees/ holds git worktrees of this repo — committing those into
# the repo they check out is incoherent.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/
